<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#linear-b">Glossary</a> · Term · script</p><h1 class="enc-title">Linear B</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1450–1200 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century-bce">15th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Linear B is the syllabic script used by the Mycenaean palaces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> from about 1450 to 1200 BCE to record an early form of Greek. Adapted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-a">Linear A</a>, it survives on clay tablets from Knossos, Pylos, Mycenae, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> and other sites, listing goods, workers, land and offerings. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-ventris">Michael Ventris</a> deciphered it in 1952.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/linear-b.jpg" alt="Linear B" width="320" height="143" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sharon Mollerus · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NAMA_Linear_B_tablet_of_Pylos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
